directAnswer:
Your belief is partly right.

Right: the CSS is the largest cost on the main thread. I gave the same page an empty style sheet. With the CPU slowed 4 times, the history page (1,116 elements) went from 1,521 ms of main thread work to 543 ms, and the inbox page from 816 ms to 384 ms. So 53% to 67% of the work of a page is CSS work (measured).

Wrong: it is not because the generated file is huge. Chrome parses the 475 KB sheet in about 13 ms (CPU slowed 4 times). A sheet with only the classes of the page (170 KB) gave no stable gain: 0.97 to 1.42 times in four runs, with ranges that overlap (measured). The cost is in the rules that the elements do use.

So the four causes:

1. Something that the lab does and does not need: yes, three things, and none is the main cost.
- The gallery shows 10 live preview frames. Each frame is a full document. Without the frames the gallery needs 818 ms of main thread work, with them 3,792 ms (measured, CPU slowed 4 times). The frames are 78% of the gallery.
- Tailwind scans apps/lab/audit, apps/lab/docs, and apps/lab/README.md. A write to one of these files makes the Tailwind plugin send `full-reload` to each open document (measured: 4 of 4 writes on one preview, 10 to 11 messages on the gallery). The fix is one line, and it also removes 44,747 B:

```css
/* apps/lab/src/styles.css */
@import "tailwindcss" source("./");
```

With that line: 0 messages and 0 requests in 4 of 4 writes (measured). This happens only when an agent writes a document, so it is occasional.
- The shared server was never restarted. Its sheet has 1,171 rules (88,556 B) for classes that are in no file now (counted). The cost in time is small.

2. The Ariakit UI primitives: yes. This is the main cost, and the real app has it too.
- Size: the recipes are 73% to 76% of the sheet (337,784 B to 355,565 B, counted). It is a fixed cost: Tailwind reads each file in src/components/ariakit/styles as text and compiles each class of each variant.
- Time: I removed one mechanism at a time from the sheet (measured, history page, style recalculation, CPU slowed 4 times): all 247 `@property` rules: 752 ms to 231 ms. Only the 51 typed `@property` rules of @ariakit/tailwind: 752 ms to 313 ms. The 30 of ui.css: to 597 ms. The 78 of Tailwind: to 691 ms. Selectors with `:has()`: no change. Size containers: no change. Style queries: 11% to 32% down. The inbox page gave the same picture (287 ms to 125 ms without the 51 rules).

The 51 rules look like this (node_modules/@ariakit/tailwind/src/output.css, line 1654):

```css
@property --_ak-lib {
  syntax: "<color>";
  inherits: false;
  initial-value: canvas;
}
```

The page needs them: without them the layer colors and the frames are gone (I took a picture). So this is not a fix. It shows where the time is: Chrome computes the layer, text, and frame formulas (`oklch(from ...)`, `calc()`, `clamp()`) as typed values for each element. Only a change in @ariakit/tailwind can lower that.

3. The development server and the tool stack: yes, for the wall time in development. One bare preview makes 289 requests and 18.9 MB. With an empty sheet, 287 requests and 18.3 MB stay, and the last request ends at the same time (2.3 s against 2.5 s, load average about 500). The main thread work goes from 553 ms to 364 ms (measured, no throttle). Also, each source edit makes Vite send `css-update` to each open document: 11 documents on the gallery fetch and apply the 517 KB sheet again (counted). Tailwind itself is fast: 58 ms for one generation (measured, median of 10).

4. Something else: this machine. The load average was 51 to 659 during this check. Any page is slow at that load. I cannot say how much of what you saw was the machine (assumption: a large part).

In short: the lab does one unnecessary thing in the CSS path (the scan of its documents), and it multiplies the cost with frames. The size of the CSS is not the problem. The work that the primitive rules give the browser for each element is the problem, and it will go to the real app with the design.

realApp:
What follows for apps/web when the settled design is implemented there.

1. The file size is not a risk. Today apps/web has 332,367 B of different rules (counted, confirmed), and 275,037 B of them come from the folder src/components/ariakit. The research estimate for the new design is 317,000 to 400,000 B raw, 32 to 38 KB with brotli (estimate, not run again). One cached request of that size costs about 13 ms of parse with the CPU slowed 4 times (measured).

2. The risk is the count of elements that use the primitives. Each element costs about 0.13 ms of style recalculation on this machine with no throttle, and about 0.7 ms with the CPU slowed 4 times (measured on the history page of the lab: 1,116 elements, 143 to 149 ms and 751 ms). The audit says that a history table of 100 runs in the real app is 1.0 MB of markup. So: limit the rows that a list renders (paging or a window), and measure the long lists first.

3. This cost cannot be removed in apps/web. It is in @ariakit/tailwind: 56% to 58% of the style time goes away when its 51 typed `@property` rules are removed (measured), but the colors and frames need them. A change there helps the real app, the lab, and every other app that uses the package.

4. Do not copy the `@source not` lines of the report into apps/web. As it is today, apps/web has no tabs.ts and no choice.ts, and src/routes/index.tsx uses table.ts (16,110 B that the page needs). The safe rule is the one that apps/web already follows: copy only the primitive files that a page uses.

5. Two small changes in apps/web are still correct:

```css
/* the one entry that stays */
@import "tailwindcss" source("./");
@import "./components/ariakit/styles/ui.css";
```

- Compile the sheet one time (audit finding FE-09). The build has 581 duplicate rules, 114,948 B (counted, confirmed; the audit number of 254 rules is the same thing counted at the top level, where I count 255).
- `source("./")` adds or removes 0 B there (counted). It only stops a possible reload when CHANGELOG.md, a migration, or a tooling file changes (documented in the plugin source, not tested in apps/web).

6. In development, the real app has 1 document, so the factor 11 of the gallery is not there. One source edit costs about 40 to 60 ms of style work for one document (measured in the lab copy). The many module requests of the development server stay; that is the subject of the other lane.
